clean_summary:
SWE-ZERO uses @KLieret and my mini-SWE-agent as the agent harness. It's absolutely mindblowing how much SWE-bench data has scaled. A year ago, SWE-smith had 50k instances across 128 repositories, but @kevin_x_li's SWE-Zero blows this out of the water.

@kevin_x_li: "Introducing SWE-ZERO-12M-trajectories: the largest agentic trace dataset in the open, 5.7x larger than the previous largest. 112B tokens, 12M trajectories, 122K PRs, 3K repos, and 16 languages."

The SWE-ZERO-12M dataset significantly advances the scale of agentic trace data available for research and development in artificial intelligence.

Great read! SQLite is also represented in ProgramBench, but in this case study, the model is given a massive PRD, no access to a reference executable.

The many long horizon coding benchmarks released recently make it an opportune time to study effects of multi-agent.

@cursor_ai: "We had a team of agents rebuild SQLite from its 835-page manual. It created a replica in Rust which passed 100% of a held-out test suite. Interestingly, cost varied 15x depending on which model mix we used."

Lebron's going to Philadelphia. Also, Claude Opus 4.8 xhigh sets a record high for almost resolved instances at 16.5% for ProgramBench.

In the Anthropic family of models, this is a significant leap, progressing from Opus 4.6 to 4.8-xhigh with substantial improvements in resolution performance.

